cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or data, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Shadow Node Theory v2.7.1: Scale-Invariant Satellization, Coupled Orbital Collapse, and the Integrated Uncertainty Fabric"
abstract: >
  Replication package for Shadow Node Theory v2.7.1 (SNT v2.7.1), a formal
  model of node satellization with a corpus of 721 empirical cases
  reconstructed from verifiable primary sources and a coupled Orbital
  Collapse layer (ACO-A). This release adds an integrated uncertainty-fabric
  layer: 699 real, source-versioned trajectories and 41 explicitly labeled
  model shadows, totaling 740 trajectories and 51,210 points. The exogenous
  series, cube implementation, cymatic analyses, figures, derived data, and
  audits are included. The 721-case SNT corpus itself is unchanged; the
  trajectory count is a separate analytical layer, not 740 independent
  replications. The active conceptual framework is marco teórico v34,
  numbered independently from the software release and corpus.
  The published arithmetic replicates; the inference does not always. The
  friction finding keeps its direction (per case rho = -0.68, n = 714) but is
  not significant at the domain level (rho = -0.56, p = 0.25) and is not
  supported by a pre-registered test with new non-COVID domains
  (rho = -0.13, p = 0.39); the friction-free (epidemic) pole is real and
  robust to autocorrelation when rebuilt from raw OWID series. Domain B
  (country pairs) reproduces byte for byte from the Maddison Project
  Database 2020 but does not measure hub-node coupling: a discriminant test
  with bilateral trade and reconstructions with trade-emergent hubs (fixed
  and time-varying) show convergence rather than satellization. A
  pre-registered test on blind-coded abrupt triggers (capital relocations
  and 1980 Special Economic Zones) supports faster satellization after
  abrupt decrees (8/8 cases, p = 0.004). In the collapse layer,
  orthogonality of satellization and collapse holds with 242 crypto pairs,
  and a positive hazard ("no system is eternal") holds in 663 crypto pairs
  and 27,771 FDIC banks; a hazard rising with age holds only in crypto.
  In the uncertainty fabric, u remains without an operational definition
  and is stored as NULL; friction phi is postulated by domain, not measured
  continuously. The leave-one-out results are exploratory and not a
  calibrated classifier.
